        "item": {
          "clientId": null,
          "content": [
            {
              "text": "{\"problem\": {\"theorem\": \"2. Show that there are infinitely many pairs of positive integers $(m, n)$ such that\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{m+1}{n}+\\\\frac{n+1}{m}\\n$$\\nis a positive integer.\", \"assumptions\": [], \"domain\": \"british_mo\", \"explicit_subgoals\": []}, \"candidate_text\": \"Define integer sequences by\\n\\\\[\\n(t_0,y_0)=(1,1),\\\\qquad t_{k+1}=2t_k+3y_k,\\\\qquad y_{k+1}=t_k+2y_k.\\n\\\\]\\nSince\\n\\\\[\\n(2t+3y)^2-3(t+2y)^2=t^2-3y^2,\\n\\\\]\\ninduction gives\\n\\\\[\\nt_k^2-3y_k^2=-2\\n\\\\]\\nfor every \\\\(k\\\\ge0\\\\). Moreover, \\\\(t_k,y_k\\\\) are positive odd integers: this is true initially and is preserved by the recurrence. Also\\n\\\\(y_{k+1}=t_k+2y_k>y_k\\\\), so the \\\\(y_k\\\\) are strictly increasing.\\n\\nSet\\n\\\\[\\nn_k=\\\\frac{y_k+1}{2},\\\\qquad m_k=\\\\frac{4n_k-1+t_k}{2}.\\n\\\\]\\nBoth are positive integers, since \\\\(y_k\\\\) and \\\\(t_k\\\\) are odd. From \\\\(t_k^2=3y_k^2-2\\\\) and \\\\(y_k=2n_k-1\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\nt_k^2=12n_k^2-12n_k+1.\\n\\\\]\\nThis is the discriminant of\\n\\\\[\\nm^2+(1-4n_k)m+(n_k^2+n_k)=0.\\n\\\\]\\nThus the quadratic formula shows that \\\\(m_k\\\\) satisfies this equation. Hence\\n\\\\[\\nm_k^2+n_k^2+m_k+n_k=4m_kn_k.\\n\\\\]\\nDividing by the positive integer \\\\(m_kn_k\\\\) yields\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{m_k optional +1}{n_k}+\\\\frac{n_k+1}{m_k}=4,\\n\\\\]\\nwhere the intended first numerator is \\\\(m_k+1\\\\); explicitly,\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{m_k+1}{n_k}+\\\\frac{n_k+1}{m_k}=4.\\n\\\\]\\nFinally, the strictly increasing values of \\\\(y_k\\\\) give strictly increasing values of \\\\(n_k\\\\), so the pairs \\\\((m_k,n_k)\\\\) are all distinct. Therefore there are infinitely many such pairs of positive integers.\"}",
              "text_elements": [],
              "type": "text"
            }
          ],
          "id": "01a094f9-1be1-7e60-ad5c-5897d70d7a62",
          "type": "userMessage"
